.Dd November 14, 2026
.Dt SOJA 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm soja
.Nd IRC bouncer, a translation of soju into Ada
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl debug
.Op Fl config Ar path
.Op Fl listen Ar uri ...
.Nm
.Fl h | help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
soja is a user-friendly IRC bouncer, written in Ada as a drop-in replacement for soju: it reads soju's configuration file and database, speaks the same IRC extensions and has the same BouncerServ commands.
See \fIdifferences.md\fP in the documentation for what is not the same.
It connects to upstream IRC servers on behalf of the user to provide extra features.
.Bl -bullet
.It
Multiple separate users sharing the same bouncer, each with their own upstream servers
.It
Sending the backlog (messages received while the user was disconnected from the bouncer), with per-client buffers
.El
.Pp
To connect to the bouncer, use the bouncer username and password.
To use a client which doesn't support the \fIsoju.im/bouncer-networks\fP IRC extension, setup one connection per server configured in soja, and indicate the network name in the username: "<username>/<network>".
Then channels can be joined and parted as if you were directly connected to the upstream server.
.Pp
For per-client history to work on clients which don't support the IRCv3 \fIchathistory\fP extension, clients need to indicate their name.
This can be done by adding a "@<client>" suffix to the username.
.Pp
When joining a channel, the channel will be saved and automatically joined on the next connection.
When registering or authenticating with NickServ, the credentials will be saved and automatically used on the next connection if the server supports SASL.
When parting a channel with the reason "detach", the channel will be detached instead of being left.
.Pp
If a network specified in the username doesn't exist, and the network name is a valid hostname, the network will be automatically added.
.Pp
When all clients are disconnected from the bouncer, the user is automatically marked as away by default.
.Pp
soja will reload the configuration file, the TLS certificate/key and the MOTD file when it receives the HUP signal.
The configuration options \fIlisten\fP, \fIdb\fP and \fIlog\fP cannot be reloaded.
.Pp
Administrators can broadcast a message to all bouncer users via \fI/notice $<hostname> <text>\fP, or via \fI/notice $* <text>\fP if the connection isn't bound to a particular network.
All currently connected bouncer users will receive the message from the special \fIBouncerServ\fP service.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy "-h, -help"
.Pp
Show help message and quit.
.It Sy -config Ar path
.Pp
Path to the config file.
If unset, a default config file is used.
.It Sy -debug
.Pp
Enable debug logging (this will leak sensitive information such as passwords).
.It Sy -listen Ar uri
.Pp
Listening URI (default: ":6697").
Can be specified multiple times.
.El
.Sh CONFIG FILE
The config file has one directive per line.
.Pp
Example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
listen ircs://
tls cert.pem key.pem
hostname example.org
.Ed
.Pp
The following directives are supported:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy listen Ar uri
.Pp
Listening URI (default: ":6697").
.Pp
The following URIs are supported:
.Bl -bullet
.It
\fI[ircs://][host][:port]\fP listens with TLS over TCP (default port if omitted: 6697)
.It
\fIirc+insecure://[host][:port]\fP listens with plain-text over TCP (default port if omitted: 6667)
.It
\fIunix://<path>\fP listens on a Unix domain socket
.It
\fIhttps://[host][:port]\fP listens for HTTPS connections (default port: 443) and handles WebSocket requests at endpoint \fI/socket\fP
.It
\fIhttp+insecure://[host][:port]\fP listens for plain-text HTTP connections (default port: 80) and handles WebSocket requests at endpoint \fI/socket\fP
.It
\fIhttp+unix://<path>\fP listens for plain-text HTTP connections on a Unix domain socket and handles WebSocket requests at endpoint \fI/socket\fP
.It
\fIwss://[host][:port]\fP listens for WebSocket connections over TLS (default port: 443)
.It
\fIws+insecure://[host][:port]\fP listens for plain-text WebSocket connections (default port: 80)
.It
\fIws+unix://<path>\fP listens for plain-text WebSocket connections on a Unix domain socket
.It
\fIident://[host][:port]\fP listens for plain-text ident connections (default port: 113)
.It
\fIhttp+prometheus://localhost:<port>\fP listens for plain-text HTTP connections and serves Prometheus metrics (host must be "localhost")
.It
\fIunix+admin://[path]\fP listens on a Unix domain socket for administrative connections, such as sojactl (default path: /run/soju/admin)
.El
.Pp
If the scheme is omitted, "ircs" is assumed.
If multiple \fBlisten\fP directives are specified, soja will listen on each of them.
.It Sy hostname Ar name
.Pp
Server hostname (default: system hostname).
.Pp
This should be set to a fully qualified domain name.
.It Sy title Ar title
.Pp
Server title.
This will be sent as the \fIISUPPORT NETWORK\fP value when clients don't select a specific network.
.It Sy tls Ar cert Ar key
.Pp
Enable TLS support.
The certificate and the key files must be PEM-encoded.
.It Sy db Ar driver Ar source
.Pp
Set the database location for user, network and channel storage.
By default, a \fIsqlite3\fP database is opened in "./soju.db".
.Pp
The supported driver is \fIsqlite3\fP: \fIsource\fP is a path to the SQLite file.
.It Sy message-store Ar driver Op Ar source
.Pp
Set the database location for IRC messages.
By default, \fIdb\fP is used.
.Pp
Supported drivers:
.Bl -bullet
.It
\fImemory\fP stores messages in memory.
For each channel/user, only the latest 4K messages are kept in memory, older messages are discarded.
This driver is very basic and doesn't support features such as the \fIchathistory\fP extension and search.
.It
\fIfs\fP stores messages on disk, in the same format as ZNC.
\fIsource\fP is required and is the root directory path for the database.
This on-disk format is lossy: some IRCv3 messages (e.g. TAGMSG) and all message tags are discarded.
.It
\fIdb\fP stores messages in the database.
A full-text search index is used to speed up search queries.
.El
.Pp
(\fIlog\fP is a deprecated alias for this directive.)
.It Sy file-upload Ar driver Op Ar source
.Pp
Set the database location for uploaded files.
.Pp
File upload requires setting up an HTTP listener (see \fIhttps://\fP and \fIhttp+insecure://\fP URIs in the \fIlisten\fP directive).
.Pp
Supported drivers:
.Bl -bullet
.It
\fIfs\fP stores uploaded files on disk.
\fIsource\fP is required.
.El
.It Sy http-origin Ar patterns...
.Pp
List of allowed HTTP origins for WebSocket listeners.
The parameters are interpreted as shell patterns, see \fBglob\fP(7).
.Pp
By default, only the request host is authorized.
Use this directive to enable cross-origin WebSockets.
.It Sy http-ingress Ar url
.Pp
External URL on which HTTPS listeners are exposed.
.Pp
By default, this is \fIhttps://<hostname>\fP.
.It Sy accept-proxy-ip Ar cidr...
.Pp
Allow the specified IPs to act as a proxy.
Proxys have the ability to overwrite the remote and local connection addresses (via the PROXY protocol, the Forwarded HTTP header field defined in RFC 7239 or the X-Forwarded-* HTTP header fields).
The special name "localhost" accepts the loopback addresses 127.0.0.0/8 and ::1/128.
.Pp
By default, all IPs are rejected.
.It Sy max-user-networks Ar limit
.Pp
Maximum number of networks per user.
By default, there is no limit.
.It Sy motd Ar path
.Pp
Path to the MOTD file.
The bouncer MOTD is sent to clients which aren't bound to a specific network.
By default, no MOTD is sent.
.It Sy upstream-user-ip Ar cidr...
.Pp
Enable per-user IP addresses.
One IPv4 range and/or one IPv6 range can be specified in CIDR notation.
One IP address per range will be assigned to each user and will be used as the source address when connecting to an upstream network.
.Pp
This can be useful to avoid having the whole bouncer banned from an upstream network because of one malicious user.
.Pp
soja reads this directive but does not yet choose the source address of upstream connections: they leave from the default address.
.It Sy disable-inactive-user Ar duration
.Pp
Disable inactive users after the specified duration.
.Pp
A user is inactive when the last downstream connection is closed.
.Pp
The duration is a positive decimal number followed by the unit "d" (days).
For instance, "30d" disables users 30 days after they last disconnect from the bouncer.
.It Sy enable-user-on-auth true|false
.Pp
Enable users when they successfully authenticate.
.Pp
This can be used together with \fIdisable-inactive-user\fP to seamlessly disable and re-enable users during lengthy inactivity.
.Pp
When external authentication is used (e.g. \fIauth oauth2\fP), bouncer users are automatically created after successfull authentication.
.It Sy auth Ar driver No ...
.Pp
Set the authentication method.
By default, internal authentication is used.
.Pp
Supported drivers:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy "auth internal"
.Pp
Use internal authentication.
.It Sy "auth oauth2" Ar url
.Pp
Use external OAuth 2.0 authentication.
The authorization server URL must be provided.
The client ID and client secret can be provided as username and password in the URL.
The authorization server must support OAuth 2.0 Authorization Server Metadata (RFC 8414) and OAuth 2.0 Token Introspection (RFC 7662).
.It Sy "auth pam"
.Pp
Use PAM authentication, through the PAM service "soju" (install a file of that name in /etc/pam.d, for instance one that includes the "login" service). libpam is loaded when soja starts; if it is not installed, soja refuses to start with this driver.
.El
.El
.Sh IRC SERVICE
soja exposes an IRC service called \fBBouncerServ\fP to manage the bouncer.
Commands can be sent via regular private messages (\fI/msg BouncerServ <command> [args...]\fP).
Commands may be written in full or abbreviated form, for instance \fBnetwork\fP can be abbreviated as \fBnet\fP or just \fBn\fP.
.Pp
Commands are parsed according the POSIX shell rules.
In particular, words can be quoted (via double or single quotes) and a backslash escapes the next character.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy help Op Ar command
.Pp
Show a list of commands.
If \fIcommand\fP is specified, show a help message for the command.
.It Sy "network create" Sy -addr Ar addr Op Ar options...
.Pp
Connect to a new network at \fIaddr\fP.
\fI-addr\fP is mandatory.
.Pp
\fIaddr\fP supports several connection types:
.Bl -bullet
.It
\fI[ircs://]<host>[:port]\fP connects with TLS over TCP
.It
\fIirc+insecure://<host>[:port]\fP connects with plain-text TCP
.It
\fIirc+unix:///<path>\fP connects to a Unix socket
.El
.Pp
For example, to connect to Libera Chat:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
net create -addr irc.libera.chat
.Ed
.Pp
Other options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -name Ar name
.Pp
Short network name.
This will be used instead of \fIaddr\fP to refer to the network.
.It Sy -username Ar username
.Pp
Connect with the specified username.
By default, the nickname is used.
.It Sy -pass Ar pass
.Pp
Connect with the specified server password.
.It Sy -realname Ar realname
.Pp
Connect with the specified real name.
By default, the account's realname is used if set, otherwise the network's nickname is used.
.It Sy -certfp Ar fingerprint
.Pp
Instead of using certificate authorities to check the server's TLS certificate, check whether the server certificate matches the provided fingerprint.
This can be used to connect to servers using self-signed certificates.
The fingerprint format is SHA512.
An empty string removes any previous fingerprint.
.Pp
The following command can be used to fetch the certificate fingerprint of an IRC server:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
openssl s_client -connect irc.example.org:6697 -verify_quiet </dev/null | openssl x509 -fingerprint -sha512 -noout -in /dev/stdin
.Ed
.It Sy -nick Ar nickname
.Pp
Connect with the specified nickname.
By default, the account's username is used.
.It Sy -auto-away true|false
.Pp
Enable or disable the auto-away feature.
If the feature is enabled, the user will be marked as away when all clients are disconnected from the bouncer.
By default, auto-away is enabled.
.It Sy -enabled true|false
.Pp
Enable or disable the network.
If the network is disabled, the bouncer won't connect to it.
By default, the network is enabled.
.It Sy -connect-command Ar command
.Pp
Send the specified quoted string as a raw IRC command right after connecting to the server.
This can be used to identify to an account when the server doesn't support SASL.
.Pp
For instance, to identify with \fINickServ\fP, the following command can be used:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
"PRIVMSG NickServ :IDENTIFY <password>"
.Ed
.Pp
The flag can be specified multiple times to send multiple IRC messages.
To clear all commands, set it to the empty string.
.El
.It Sy "network update" Op Ar name Op Ar options...
.Pp
Update an existing network.
The options are the same as the \fInetwork create\fP command.
.Pp
When this command is executed, soja will disconnect and re-connect to the network.
.Pp
If \fIname\fP is not specified, the current network is updated.
.It Sy "network delete" Op Ar name
.Pp
Disconnect and delete a network.
.Pp
If \fIname\fP is not specified, the current network is deleted.
.It Sy "network quote" Op Ar name Ar command
.Pp
Send a raw IRC line as-is to a network.
.Pp
If \fIname\fP is not specified, the command is sent to the current network.
.It Sy "network status"
.Pp
Show a list of saved networks and their current status.
.It Sy "channel status" Op Ar options...
.Pp
Show a list of saved channels and their current status.
.Pp
Options:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -network Ar name
.Pp
Only show channels for the specified network.
By default, only the channels in the current network are displayed.
.El
.It Sy "channel update" Ar name Op Ar options...
.Pp
Update the options of an existing channel.
.Pp
Options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -detached true|false
.Pp
Attach or detach this channel.
.Pp
A detached channel is joined but is hidden by the bouncer.
This is useful to e.g. collect logs and highlights in low-interest or high-traffic channels.
.It Sy -relay-detached Ar mode
.Pp
Set when to relay messages from detached channels to the user with a BouncerServ NOTICE.
.Pp
Modes are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy message
.Pp
Relay any message from this channel when detached.
.It Sy highlight
.Pp
Relay only messages mentioning you when detached.
.It Sy none
.Pp
Don't relay any messages from this channel when detached.
.It Sy default
.Pp
Currently same as \fBhighlight\fP.
This is the default behaviour.
.El
.It Sy -reattach-on Ar mode
.Pp
Set when to automatically reattach to detached channels.
.Pp
Modes are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy message
.Pp
Reattach to this channel when any message is received.
.It Sy highlight
.Pp
Reattach to this channel when any message mentioning you is received.
.It Sy none
.Pp
Never automatically reattach to this channel.
.It Sy default
.Pp
Currently same as \fBnone\fP.
This is the default behaviour.
.El
.It Sy -detach-after Ar duration
.Pp
Automatically detach this channel after the specified duration has elapsed without receving any message corresponding to \fB-detach-on\fP.
.Pp
Example duration values: \fB1h30m\fP, \fB30s\fP, \fB2.5h\fP.
.Pp
Setting this value to 0 will disable this behaviour, i.e. this channel will never be automatically detached.
This is the default behaviour.
.It Sy -detach-on Ar mode
.Pp
Set when to reset the auto-detach timer used by \fB-detach-after\fP, causing it to wait again for the auto-detach duration timer before detaching.
Joining, reattaching, sending a message, or changing any channel option will reset the timer, in addition to the messages specified by the mode.
.Pp
Modes are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy message
.Pp
Receiving any message from this channel will reset the auto-detach timer.
.It Sy highlight
.Pp
Receiving any message mentioning you from this channel will reset the auto-detach timer.
.It Sy none
.Pp
Receiving messages from this channel will not reset the auto-detach timer.
Sending messages or joining the channel will still reset the timer.
.It Sy default
.Pp
Currently same as \fBmessage\fP.
This is the default behaviour.
.El
.El
.It Sy "channel delete" Ar name
.Pp
Leave and forget a channel.
.It Sy "certfp generate" Op Ar options...
.Pp
Generate a certificate and use it for authentication (via SASL EXTERNAL).
.Pp
Generates a 3072-bit RSA private key by default.
The certificate is valid for 20 years.
.Pp
Options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -network Ar name
.Pp
Select a network.
By default, the current network is selected, if any.
.It Sy -key-type Ar type
.Pp
Private key algorithm to use.
Valid values are: \fIrsa\fP, \fIecdsa\fP and \fIed25519\fP.
\fIecdsa\fP uses the NIST P-384 curve (soju uses P-521).
.It Sy -bits Ar bits
.Pp
Size of RSA key to generate: 2048, 3072 or 4096 (soju accepts any size up to 8192).
Ignored for other key types.
.El
.Pp
The new certificate is used the next time the network connects; use \fBnetwork update\fP to reconnect now.
.It Sy "certfp fingerprint" Op Ar options...
.Pp
Show SHA-1 and SHA-256 fingerprints for the certificate currently used with the network.
.Pp
Options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -network Ar name
.Pp
Select a network.
By default, the current network is selected, if any.
.El
.It Sy "sasl status" Op Ar options...
.Pp
Show current SASL status.
.Pp
Options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -network Ar name
.Pp
Select a network.
By default, the current network is selected, if any.
.El
.It Sy "sasl set-plain" Op Ar options... Ar username Ar password
.Pp
Set SASL PLAIN credentials.
.Pp
Options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -network Ar name
.Pp
Select a network.
By default, the current network is selected, if any.
.El
.It Sy "sasl reset" Op Ar options...
.Pp
Disable SASL authentication and remove stored credentials.
.Pp
Options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -network Ar name
.Pp
Select a network.
By default, the current network is selected, if any.
.El
.It Sy "user status" Op Ar username
.Pp
Show a list of users on this server.
Only admins can query this information.
.Pp
If \fIusername\fP is specified, statistics are only displayed for this user.
.It Sy "user create" -username Ar username -password Ar password Op Ar options...
.Pp
Create a new soja user.
Only admin users can create new accounts.
The \fI-username\fP and \fI-password\fP flags are mandatory.
.Pp
Options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -username Ar username
.Pp
The bouncer username.
This cannot be changed after the user has been created.
.It Sy -password Ar password
.Pp
The bouncer password.
.It Sy -disable-password
.Pp
Disable password authentication.
The user will be unable to login.
.It Sy -admin true|false
.Pp
Make the new user an administrator.
.It Sy -nick Ar nick
.Pp
Set the user's nickname.
This is used as a fallback if there is no nickname set for a network.
.It Sy -realname Ar realname
.Pp
Set the user's realname.
This is used as a fallback if there is no realname set for a network.
.It Sy -enabled true|false
.Pp
Enable or disable the user.
If the user is disabled, the bouncer will not connect to any of their networks, and downstream connections will be immediately closed.
By default, users are enabled.
.El
.It Sy "user update" Op Ar username Op Ar options...
.Pp
Update a user.
The options are the same as the \fIuser create\fP command.
.Pp
If \fIusername\fP is omitted, the current user is updated.
Only admins can update other users.
.Pp
Not all flags are valid in all contexts:
.Bl -bullet
.It
The \fI-username\fP flag is never valid, usernames are immutable.
.It
The \fI-nick\fP and \fI-realname\fP flag are only valid when updating the current user.
.It
The \fI-admin\fP and \fI-enabled\fP flags are only valid when updating another user.
.El
.It Sy "user delete" Ar username Op Ar "confirmation token"
.Pp
Delete a soja user.
.Pp
Only admins can delete other users.
.It Sy "user run" Ar username Ar command...
.Pp
Execute a command as another user.
.Pp
Only admins can use this command.
.It Sy "server status"
.Pp
Show some bouncer statistics.
Only admins can query this information.
.It Sy "server notice" Ar message
.Pp
Broadcast a notice.
All currently connected bouncer users will receive the message from the special \fIBouncerServ\fP service.
Only admins can broadcast a notice.
.El
